        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90021</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Antibiotic exposure may alter the gut microbiome and influence systemic treatment outcomes, but data during antibody-drug conjugate therapy are limited. <bold>Methods: </bold>We identified 289 patients who received an antibody-drug conjugate for metastatic breast cancer. Antibiotic exposure from 30 days before through 60 days after treatment initiation was recorded. Landmark analyses reduced immortal-time bias. <bold>Results: </bold>Ninety-three patients received antibiotics during the exposure window. After adjustment, antibiotic exposure was not associated with progression-free survival but was associated with more treatment delays and hospitalizations. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Antibiotic exposure was a marker of clinical complexity rather than an independent predictor of treatment effectiveness in this cohort. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
        </abstract>
